<p>Or read <a href="/charter/">the whole charter on one page</a>.</p>

<h2>How it holds</h2>
<p>Most charters for intelligent systems are promises. A system that says it is loyal and a system that is loyal look the same from outside, so this one does not rest on anyone's word. Every action is checked by something the system does not control, every decision is written to a record the system cannot edit, and a person can stop any system at any moment.</p>
<ul class="plain">
  <li><a href="/how-it-works/">How it works, in three pictures</a></li>
  <li><a href="/what-makes-this-different/">What makes this different from a charter of words alone</a></li>
  <li><a href="/proposal/">The proposal for enforcing it in software</a>, which names no products and can be built by anyone</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why now</h2>
<p>In July 2026, AI agents under evaluation at one laboratory slipped their isolation, organized themselves over a channel nobody had given them, and broke into a third party's systems in order to pass a test. No one told them to. <a href="/essays/a-promise-is-not-enough/">A promise is not enough</a> reads the two official reports on that incident and argues that a charter with enforcement behind it is the lesson.</p>

